{"step_index":156,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-01T18:58:00Z","content":"I checked the available NexLev tools, and there is no way to *input* custom geographic percentages into NexLev to see how it changes the RPM output. The `get_geography_revenue` tool only accepts a Channel ID and returns whatever data NexLev's model generates.\n\nHowever, since we know how YouTube ad revenue works mathematically, we don't need NexLev to calculate this! I can build a matrix for you based on standard industry RPMs for different countries.\n\nLet's do the math for both **FactTechz** (92.2M long-form views) and **NeonMan** (8.8M long-form views) using realistic geographic splits.\n\n### The Baseline Assumptions (Standard YouTube Ad Rates)\nHere is roughly what advertisers pay per 1,000 views (RPM) depending on where the viewer lives:\n*   **Tier 1 (US, UK, Canada, Australia):** ~$5.00 RPM (Often higher, but keeping it conservative)\n*   **Tier 2 (Europe, Middle East):** ~$2.00 RPM\n*   **Tier 3 (India, Pakistan, Bangladesh):** ~$0.40 RPM (Standard for general Hindi content)\n\n---\n\n### Matrix 1: FactTechz (92.2 Million Monthly Long-Form Views)\n\nHere is how FactTechz's revenue changes based on where the audience *actually* is:\n\n| Geographic Split | Tier 1 (US/UK) Rev | Tier 2 Rev | Tier 3 (India) Rev | **Total Monthly Rev** | **Effective RPM** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **NexLev's Template** (46% US, 22% India) | $211,000 | $0 (ignored) | $8,000 | **$389,785** | **$4.31** |\n| **Realistic Diaspora** (20% T1, 5% T2, 75% India) | $92,200 | $9,220 | $27,660 | **$129,080** | **$1.40** |\n| **Heavy India** (10% T1, 2% T2, 88% India) | $46,100 | $3,688 | $32,454 | **$82,242** | **$0.89** |\n| **Extreme India** (3% T1, 2% T2, 95% India) | $13,830 | $3,688 | $35,036 | **$52,554** | **$0.57** |\n\n**Conclusion for FactTechz:** Even if we assume a massive 95% India audience, 92.2 million views is so huge that they are still pulling in **over $50,000 a month** just from AdSense. But it's far from the $389K NexLev hallucinates. \n\n---\n\n### Matrix 2: NeonMan (8.8 Million Monthly Long-Form Views)\n\nNeonMan is Hindi gossip/drama. The Indian diaspora in the US doesn't care much about \"India's Got Latent\" beef, so his audience is almost certainly heavily concentrated in India.\n\n| Geographic Split | Tier 1 (US/UK) Rev | Tier 2 Rev | Tier 3 (India) Rev | **Total Monthly Rev** | **Effective RPM** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **NexLev's Template** (40% US, 23% India) | $17,600 | $0 (ignored) | $800 | **$15,144** | **$1.72** |\n| **Heavy India** (10% T1, 2% T2, 88% India) | $4,400 | $352 | $3,097 | **$7,849** | **$0.89** |\n| **Extreme India** (3% T1, 2% T2, 95% India) | $1,320 | $352 | $3,344 | **$5,016** | **$0.57** |\n| **Pure India** (1% T1, 1% T2, 98% India) | $440 | $176 | $3,449 | **$4,065** | **$0.46** |\n\n**Conclusion for NeonMan:** NeonMan is likely earning between **$4,000 and $8,000 per month**. NexLev's $15,144 estimate is inflated by a fake US audience injection.\n\n### The Big Takeaway\n\nYou were 100% correct to spot that anomaly. NexLev cannot see real YouTube demographics unless the creator connects their channel directly to NexLev. For public channels, they use an AI model to guess. \n\nFor English channels, their guess is pretty good. For Hindi channels, their model breaks completely and assumes everyone is an American watching Hindi content, artificially inflating the RPM by 300-500%. \n\nIf you are researching niches for a new channel, **do not trust NexLev's revenue estimates for Hindi/regional channels.** Calculate it yourself using the views and an RPM of ~$0.40 - $0.80.","thinking":"**Calculating Geographic Splits**\n\nI'm currently focused on determining RPM and revenue using customized geographic breakdowns. It's crucial that I prioritize mathematical calculations over arbitrary command execution whenever possible. I am responding to the user's explicit request.\n\n\n**Estimating Geographic Distributions**\n\nI've shifted focus to manual calculations, as NexLev doesn't allow direct demographic input. I'm now estimating geography splits and revenue using industry-standard RPMs for different geos within the niches of FactTechz and NeonMan, considering a split of roughly 90-95% India and 5-10% Tier 1 countries. I'm focusing on estimating values within a ~$4.00 - $8.00 range for Tier 1 territories.\n\n\n"}
